---
import Layout from '~/layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteNav from '~/components/SiteNav.astro';
import SiteFooter from '~/components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { SectionHeading } from '~/components/SectionHeading';

// Hero screenshots are captured from Storybook into the app repo and copied to
// this site's public/ dir. See scripts/screenshot-stories.mjs in the app.
const img = (slug: string) => `/whats-new/2.0.0/${slug}.png`;

const toc = [
  { id: 'agent-overview', label: 'Agent Overview' },
  { id: 'main-window', label: 'From sidebar to a real window' },
  { id: 'settings', label: 'Settings, reimagined' },
  { id: 'pr-detail', label: 'A better PR detail' },
  { id: 'work-items', label: 'Work Items, a real workspace' },
  { id: 'sql', label: 'Faster, honest SQL' },
  { id: 'notifications', label: 'Notifications you can act on' },
  { id: 'other', label: 'Other noteworthy changes' },
  { id: 'breaking', label: 'Breaking changes' },
];

const figStyle =
  'width:100%;height:auto;display:block;border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--color-subtle-border, rgba(128,128,128,0.22));box-shadow:0 18px 50px -24px rgba(0,0,0,0.55);';
const capStyle =
  'margin-top:10px;font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-muted);text-align:center;';
const figWrap = 'margin:28px 0 36px;';
const h2Style =
  'font-size:26px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:-0.01em;color:var(--color-text-primary);margin:0 0 6px;scroll-margin-top:90px;';
const eyebrowStyle =
  'font-family:var(--font-mono,monospace);font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.14em;color:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:8px;';
const pStyle =
  'font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--color-text-secondary);margin:0 0 16px;';
const liStyle = 'font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--color-text-secondary);margin:0 0 8px;';
const sectionWrap = 'max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;padding:8px 0 18px;';
---

<Layout title="BorgDock 2.0 — From sidebar to command center">
  <SiteNav current="changelog" />

  <section class="section section--first" style="max-width:820px;">
    <SectionHeading
      eyebrow="Release · June 9, 2026"
      title={{ lead: 'BorgDock ', em: '2.0' }}
      lede="The biggest release yet. The docked sidebar grew up into a real window, a brand-new Agent Overview keeps an eye on every Claude Code session you run, and just about every screen — Settings, PR detail, Work Items, SQL — got rebuilt. Here's the tour."
    />
    <p style={`${pStyle}margin-top:24px;`}>
      If you've been running a handful of Claude Code agents at once, juggling pull requests,
      and bouncing between Azure DevOps and SQL all day — this one's for you. We pulled BorgDock
      out of the corner of your screen, gave it a proper home, and taught it to watch your agents
      so you don't have to. Let's dig in.
    </p>

    <nav
      style="margin:28px 0 0;padding:18px 20px;border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--color-subtle-border, rgba(128,128,128,0.22));"
    >
      <div style="font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:var(--color-text-muted);margin-bottom:10px;">
        In this release
      </div>
      <ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:6px 24px;">
        {
          toc.map((t) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${t.id}`} style="font-size:14px;color:var(--color-accent);text-decoration:none;">
                {t.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </section>

  {/* ── Agent Overview ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>✨ The big one</div>
    <h2 id="agent-overview" style={h2Style}>Agent Overview</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Run more than one Claude Code session at a time and you know the problem: a dozen terminals,
      and you can never remember which one is mid-task, which one finished, and which one is quietly
      waiting for you to answer a question. <strong>Agent Overview</strong> is a brand-new window
      that turns all of that into one live dashboard. Every session is a card that tells you what
      it's doing right now — <em>"Editing AgentCard.tsx"</em>, <em>"Running npm test"</em> — whether
      it's working, running a tool, just finished, idle, or waiting on you, and how much of its
      context window it has used. Open it from the tray, press <code>Ctrl+Win+Shift+A</code>, or have
      it pop up automatically when BorgDock starts.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('agent-overview')} alt="The Agent Overview dashboard" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Every Claude Code session, grouped by repo — working, finished, idle, and waiting on you.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      The part we use most: sessions that are <strong>waiting on you</strong> float to the top in a
      yellow alert rail, and the title bar shows an age that turns amber after three minutes and red
      after ten. Step away and BorgDock sends a desktop notification — "Claude needs your input" —
      with a button that raises the actual terminal running that session. No more hunting.
    </p>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Click any session and the <strong>Inspector</strong> opens, showing exactly what that agent
      touched this turn: the files it edited, wrote, or read, each with a diffstat and an expandable
      inline diff, plus its last question and your last reply. From there it's one click to focus the
      terminal, snooze the card for five minutes, or mark it seen.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('agent-overview-inspector')} alt="The session Inspector showing per-turn file diffs" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        The Inspector: what the agent changed this turn, its last message, and one-click actions.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Turning it on takes a single checkbox in Settings → Agent Overview. BorgDock wires a small
      OpenTelemetry block into your <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> (backing up the original
      first) so Claude Code streams events over loopback — <strong>nothing ever leaves your
      machine</strong> — and removes only what it added when you switch it off.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── Main window ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>🪟 A real window</div>
    <h2 id="main-window" style={h2Style}>From sidebar to a real window</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      BorgDock no longer clamps itself to the edge of your screen and slides away the moment you click
      elsewhere. It's a normal window now — drag it anywhere, resize it, minimize and maximize it, drop
      it on a second monitor — and it remembers exactly where and how you left it. Summon it from
      anywhere with <code>Ctrl+Win+Shift+G</code> (one key shows, focuses, or hides it), and closing the
      window tucks it to the tray so polling keeps running in the background.
    </p>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Inside, the single scrolling list became three tabs right in the title bar: <strong>Focus</strong>,
      <strong> PRs</strong>, and <strong>Work Items</strong>.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('main-window-prs')} alt="The main BorgDock window on the PRs tab" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        A real, resizable window. The PRs tab keeps your review queue pinned and groups the rest by repo.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      The new <strong>Focus</strong> tab is the one that earns its keep. It opens with
      "N pull requests need your attention," ranked by readiness, CI state, and review signals, with a
      one-click Start Quick Review to blast through your queue. Each PR is a clean ranked row — its
      position, a score ring, why it surfaced, and a live status — and when nothing needs you, you get
      calm instead of noise.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('main-window-focus')} alt="The Focus tab ranking the PRs that need attention" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        The Focus tab tells you what to look at next — ranked, with one-click Quick Review.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── Settings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>⚙️ Reimagined</div>
    <h2 id="settings" style={h2Style}>Settings, reimagined</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Settings moved out of the cramped flyout into a roomy, resizable window with a navigation rail
      grouped into Data sources, Application, AI, and System — and a breadcrumb that always tells you
      where you are. Every section was rewritten on a fresh set of controls.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('settings-window')} alt="The dedicated Settings window" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        A dedicated Settings window with a grouped rail and live GitHub rate-limit gauges.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ul style="margin:0 0 16px;padding-left:20px;">
      <li style={liStyle}><strong>Search every setting</strong> — press <code>Ctrl+K</code>, type, and jump straight to the field with a highlight pulse.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}><strong>Maintenance &amp; diagnostics</strong> — clear the cache, prune merged worktrees, or run a one-click self-test of your GitHub auth, Azure CLI, and cache.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}><strong>Scan a folder for repos</strong> — point BorgDock at a parent folder and it discovers every git repo and reads its remote automatically.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}><strong>See your GitHub API budget</strong> — separate live gauges for the REST and GraphQL pools, so you know your poll interval is sustainable.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('settings-search')} alt="Searching settings with Ctrl+K" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Search across every field by label, hint, and keyword — then jump right to it.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── PR detail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>🔀 Pull requests</div>
    <h2 id="pr-detail" style={h2Style}>A better PR detail</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      The PR detail window got a top-to-bottom redesign. A <strong>sticky action bar</strong> keeps
      Merge, Open in Browser, Copy Branch, Checkout, Mark Draft, Bypass, and Close PR available from
      any tab, and a persistent checks strip shows whether your builds are running, failing, or green —
      with a tap to jump to the Checks tab.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('pr-detail')} alt="PR detail with the sticky action bar and checks strip" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Every PR action in one always-visible toolbar, with a live checks summary above the tabs.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Reviews, comments, and code-anchored threads now live together in one chronological
      <strong> Discussion</strong> timeline, with filter chips to narrow to just reviews, comments, or
      on-code threads — and one-click resolve. You can write and submit a review (Approve, Comment, or
      Request changes) without leaving the window, right next to the diff, and code threads appear
      inline beneath the exact line they're about.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('pr-discussion')} alt="The unified Discussion tab" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        One Discussion timeline — reviews, comments, and code threads, with resolve built in.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── Work Items ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>📋 Azure DevOps</div>
    <h2 id="work-items" style={h2Style}>Work Items, a real workspace</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      The Work Items tab is now a full three-pane workspace: your queries on the left, matching items
      in the middle, and the full work item on the right. Click the title to rename it, click any chip
      to change state, priority, assignee, or iteration — and your edits save themselves a moment later
      with a quiet "Saved." No Save button, no modal.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('main-window-workitems')} alt="The three-pane Work Items workspace" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Queries, list, and an auto-saving editor — triage ADO work the way you triage PRs.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      And the <code>Ctrl+F9</code> palette became a proper power-user surface: type naturally —
      <code>@koen</code>, <code>state:active</code>, <code>type:bug</code> — and each operator turns
      into a live chip while the list narrows. Filter chips and group-by buttons slice a long query in
      a second, and every row is packed with signal.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('workitem-palette')} alt="The dense, filterable Work Item palette" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Chip-based filtering and grouping, with dense rows that show priority, state, and assignee.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── SQL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>🗄️ SQL</div>
    <h2 id="sql" style={h2Style}>Faster, honest SQL</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      The results grid is now virtualized, so a query that returns ten thousand rows opens, scrolls,
      and clicks as snappily as one that returns ten — and the old 30-second query cap is gone, so slow
      reports finally get the time they need.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('sql-results')} alt="Virtualized SQL results grid" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Virtualized results — huge result sets scroll and click instantly.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Run a write — an <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>MERGE</code>,
      or DDL — and BorgDock now tells you plainly how many rows it changed instead of showing an empty
      grid: a green "42 affected" pill and "42 rows affected · 18 ms."
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('sql-rows-affected')} alt="Rows-affected feedback for a SQL write" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Honest row counts for writes — instant confirmation your statement did what you expected.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── Notifications ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>🔔 Notifications</div>
    <h2 id="notifications" style={h2Style}>Notifications you can act on</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      Every alert now arrives as one polished toast near the tray, with a severity stripe, a clear
      title and body, and one-tap actions — Open PR, Fix with Claude, Merge, Start review. Click the
      card to jump to the PR, hover to pause the auto-hide, dismiss with the X. Up to three stack at
      once.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('notify-toasts')} alt="Interactive notification toasts" loading="lazy" style={figStyle} />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>
        Toasts you can act on — Fix with Claude, Start review, Merge — right from the corner.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      And when one of your PRs lands, BorgDock celebrates with you: a gold "Merged" toast with a
      shimmer, a glow, and a little rocket — plus an optional tada sound you can toggle in Settings.
    </p>
    <figure style={figWrap}>
      <img src={img('notify-merge-celebration')} alt="The merge celebration toast" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;max-width:520px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--color-subtle-border, rgba(128,128,128,0.22));box-shadow:0 18px 50px -24px rgba(0,0,0,0.55);" />
      <figcaption style={capStyle}>A little celebration when your work ships.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  {/* ── Other changes ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>✅ Also in 2.0</div>
    <h2 id="other" style={h2Style}>Other noteworthy changes</h2>
    <ul style="margin:0 0 16px;padding-left:20px;">
      <li style={liStyle}>Pop-out windows open clean (no flash of a blank window) and reopen at the size and position you left them.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}>A refreshed BorgDock app icon — a sharper purple-gradient pulse-waveform mark.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}>The tray opens the main window on left-click and gained a direct Agent Overview entry.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}>Smarter palette and SQL hotkeys (<code>Ctrl+F7</code>/<code>F8</code>/<code>F9</code>/<code>F10</code>) that hide, raise, or open without wiping your input — and open faster.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}>Markdown links in a PR open in your OS browser, not the in-app webview.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}>Cancelled CI checks no longer drag a PR red, and an "In progress" group is pinned to the top of the Checks tab.</li>
      <li style={liStyle}>Run-at-startup actually registers with the OS now, and a Test notification button confirms toasts work.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style={`${pStyle}font-size:14px;`}>
      Under the hood, the whole frontend moved to a single bun workspace, picked up the React Compiler,
      and updated to Vite 8, Vitest 4, Storybook 10, and TypeScript 6 — and every window now has a full
      Storybook catalog, which is exactly how the screenshots on this page were captured.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── Breaking ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="section" style={sectionWrap}>
    <div style={eyebrowStyle}>⚠️ Heads-up</div>
    <h2 id="breaking" style={h2Style}>Breaking changes</h2>
    <p style={pStyle}>
      BorgDock 2.0 replaces the docked, auto-hiding sidebar with a regular window you summon with a
      hotkey (<code>Ctrl+Win+Shift+G</code> by default). The old <em>sidebar edge / mode / width</em>
      settings are gone — everything they did is now handled by normal window management. There's
      nothing to migrate; just press the hotkey and BorgDock appears.
    </p>
    <div style="margin-top:32px;display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
      <a href="/download" class="site-nav__cta" style="text-decoration:none;">Download BorgDock 2.0</a>
      <a href="/changelog" style="align-self:center;font-size:14px;color:var(--color-accent);text-decoration:none;">See the full changelog →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
